---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title='Scrobbling interoperability principles — Scrobble.dev';
const description='A working, implementation-neutral set of principles for portable media scrobble events, identity, provenance, synchronization, correction, and user-controlled history.';
const schema=[{'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'TechArticle',headline:'Scrobbling interoperability principles',description,dateModified:'2026-08-11',version:'0.1-draft',author:{'@type':'Organization',name:'Scrobble.dev'}}];
---
<BaseLayout {title} {description} type="TechArticle" schema={schema}>
  <div class="shell section article">
    <article class="article-body">
      <p class="eyebrow">Working draft · 0.1</p><h1>Scrobbling interoperability principles</h1>
      <p class="lede">This is a deliberately small interoperability layer: enough shared language to exchange media activity without forcing every tracker, player, library, metadata provider, or research project into one data model.</p>
      <div class="callout"><strong>Status:</strong> discussion draft. Implementations may adopt, critique, extend, or map to it. Compatibility claims should name the version and any extensions rather than imply universal conformance.</div>
      <h2 id="model">1. Separate the domain concepts</h2>
      <p>A scrobble event, media entity, user state, rating, collection item, list membership, and derived statistic are distinct concepts. Systems may present them together but should preserve that distinction in storage and exchange.</p>
      <h2 id="event">2. Events should be reconstructable</h2>
      <p>An exchanged event should contain, or reference, enough information to answer: <em>what happened, to what, when, how much, and according to whom?</em></p>
      <p>Recommended event fields include a stable event identifier, event type, media type, subject identity, occurred-at timestamp, recorded-at timestamp, progress/completion state, provenance, and optional context.</p>
      <h2 id="identity">3. Identity is plural</h2>
      <p>No single metadata provider is authoritative for every medium. A record should be capable of carrying multiple external identifiers plus local identity. Crosswalks should preserve source and confidence instead of silently replacing IDs.</p>
      <h2 id="time">4. Time has semantics</h2>
      <p>“Watched at” can mean playback start, threshold reached, completion, manual diary date, import date, or sync receipt time. Exchanges should distinguish event time from record-ingestion time when the difference is known.</p>
      <h2 id="progress">5. Progress is medium-specific</h2>
      <p>Percent complete is useful but insufficient as a universal truth. Systems should allow native measures such as milliseconds, pages, chapters, episodes, tracks, sessions, turns, or explicit completion states and derive percentages where appropriate.</p>
      <h2 id="provenance">6. Preserve provenance</h2>
      <p>Manual entries, imports, webhooks, player telemetry, inferred events and synchronization copies have different reliability characteristics. Provenance should survive round trips so corrections and conflicts can be reasoned about later.</p>
      <h2 id="sync">7. Synchronization must be idempotent</h2>
      <p>Repeatedly receiving the same logical event should not create unlimited duplicate history. Implementations should expose enough identity or deduplication material to make safe retry possible.</p>
      <h2 id="correction">8. Corrections are first-class</h2>
      <p>Users need to amend timestamps, identity, progress and duplicate events. Systems should support deletion or tombstoning and should not require a false replacement event to correct history.</p>
      <h2 id="ownership">9. Portability is part of correctness</h2>
      <p>A tracker should provide a practical export path for user history and clearly document fields that cannot round-trip. Proprietary enhancements are compatible with this principle when a stable portable core remains available.</p>
      <h2 id="privacy">10. Minimize unnecessary telemetry</h2>
      <p>Scrobbling can reveal sensitive habits and routines. Exchange formats should avoid requiring device, location, account, or behavioral metadata that is not necessary for the requested tracking function.</p>
      <h2 id="knowledge">11. Documentation is part of the interface</h2>
      <p>Definitions, mappings, examples, known edge cases, provenance rules and version changes should be version-controlled next to implementations. Scrobble.dev publishes its knowledge layer as plain Markdown with structured metadata so humans and agents can inspect the same source.</p>
      <h2 id="extensions">12. Extend without pretending consensus</h2>
      <p>Media domains will need richer concepts. Extensions should identify their namespace or owning project, document semantics, and retain a mapping to the portable core when possible.</p>
      <h2 id="participate">Participate</h2>
      <p>The standard should develop through concrete interoperability failures: duplicate plays, ambiguous editions, anime numbering conflicts, podcast GUID changes, game platform variants, imports that lose timestamps, and similar cases. <a href="/contribute/">Contribute a case, mapping, example, or implementation note</a>.</p>
    </article>
